1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,286
Coach: An account of an experience often includes what happened afterwards.

2
00:00:04,286 --> 00:00:09,857
Coach: The useful question is not how impressive you can make that result sound,

3
00:00:09,857 --> 00:00:12,000
Coach: but what you actually know.

4
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:15,429
Coach: Separate the change you made, what you observed,

5
00:00:15,429 --> 00:00:18,429
Coach: and conclusions that would need more evidence.

6
00:00:18,779 --> 00:00:21,779
Coach: Return to our fictional visitor-guide example.

7
00:00:21,779 --> 00:00:27,779
Coach: The participant changed the map label to Blue Room and told Maya and Noah.

8
00:00:27,779 --> 00:00:32,065
Coach: Maya checked the schedule, and Noah assembled the updated guide.

9
00:00:32,065 --> 00:00:35,065
Coach: These are completed actions in the case,

10
00:00:35,065 --> 00:00:38,065
Coach: with different people responsible for different parts.

11
00:00:38,415 --> 00:00:41,844
Coach: We also know one observation from the event:

12
00:00:41,844 --> 00:00:46,986
Coach: the participant saw one visitor use the map to find Blue Room.

13
00:00:46,986 --> 00:00:50,843
Coach: That supports describing this particular use of the map.

14
00:00:50,843 --> 00:00:54,700
Coach: It is not a total count of people helped,

15
00:00:54,700 --> 00:00:58,129
Coach: or a measure of the whole event’s success.

16
00:01:06,479 --> 00:01:10,765
You: I changed the map label and told Maya and Noah.

17
00:01:10,765 --> 00:01:15,050
You: Maya checked the schedule, and Noah assembled the updated guide.

18
00:01:15,050 --> 00:01:21,050
You: During the event, I saw one visitor use the map to find Blue Room.

19
00:01:21,400 --> 00:01:25,257
Coach: Notice the difference between I saw and everyone could.

20
00:01:25,257 --> 00:01:27,829
Coach: The first reports the available observation.

21
00:01:27,829 --> 00:01:32,971
Coach: The second would make a wider claim that this case cannot support.

22
00:01:32,971 --> 00:01:35,971
Coach: Specific limits do not erase a contribution;

23
00:01:35,971 --> 00:01:39,400
Coach: they help the listener understand its actual scope.

24
00:01:39,750 --> 00:01:44,464
Coach: There was no visitor survey or total count of people helped.

25
00:01:44,464 --> 00:01:50,036
Coach: Avoid turning the single observation into a percentage, an estimate of many visitors,

26
00:01:50,036 --> 00:01:53,036
Coach: or a claim that nobody was confused.

27
00:01:53,036 --> 00:01:56,893
Coach: None of those details is available in the example.

28
00:01:57,243 --> 00:02:01,957
Coach: The label was changed before the observed visitor used the map.

29
00:02:01,957 --> 00:02:07,100
Coach: But we do not know what would have happened without the change.

30
00:02:07,100 --> 00:02:10,529
Coach: That visitor might have found the room anyway.

31
00:02:10,529 --> 00:02:15,672
Coach: The case does not provide a comparison that can settle the question.

32
00:02:16,022 --> 00:02:18,593
Coach: You can still explain the decision.

33
00:02:18,593 --> 00:02:20,736
Coach: Conflicting labels could confuse visitors,

34
00:02:20,736 --> 00:02:25,022
Coach: so the participant checked with the coordinator instead of guessing.

35
00:02:25,022 --> 00:02:28,022
Coach: That is the reasoning behind the action.

36
00:02:28,022 --> 00:02:31,450
Coach: It does not establish that confusion was measured,

37
00:02:31,450 --> 00:02:34,879
Coach: or that the change prevented it for everyone.

38
00:02:35,229 --> 00:02:39,515
You: I wanted the map label to match the door sign.

39
00:02:39,515 --> 00:02:44,229
You: I saw one visitor use the map to find the room,

40
00:02:44,229 --> 00:02:48,943
You: but I cannot say what would have happened without the change.

41
00:02:49,293 --> 00:02:51,436
Coach: This account preserves the contribution

42
00:02:51,436 --> 00:02:55,293
Coach: without making the participant responsible for the whole event.

43
00:02:55,293 --> 00:02:58,722
Coach: It also leaves room for other people’s work.

44
00:02:58,722 --> 00:03:03,436
Coach: You do not need an invented achievement to explain why checking

45
00:03:03,436 --> 00:03:07,722
Coach: an ambiguous detail was a sensible action in this case.

46
00:03:08,072 --> 00:03:11,929
Coach: Privately form a result sentence from the case facts,

47
00:03:11,929 --> 00:03:16,215
Coach: or check one from a real experience of your own.

48
00:03:16,215 --> 00:03:20,501
Coach: Which words report an observation, and which might claim more?

49
00:03:20,501 --> 00:03:25,644
Coach: Keep anything unknown visible rather than filling the gap with a number

50
00:03:25,644 --> 00:03:27,358
Coach: or a confident guess.

51
00:03:39,708 --> 00:03:42,279
Coach: A precise result can be modest.

52
00:03:42,279 --> 00:03:46,136
Coach: State what changed, give the observation you can support,

53
00:03:46,136 --> 00:03:48,279
Coach: and distinguish any wider uncertainty.

54
00:03:48,279 --> 00:03:52,565
Coach: These are useful checks when needed, not three compulsory sentences.

55
00:03:52,565 --> 00:03:57,279
Coach: Next we will connect reflection to a specific follow-up question.
